## 5. Merge Rules (Parent, After 12 Reports)

  R1  Read all 12 reports. Discard any proposed row with no disk proof.
      Proof is a named file, lock, or fail-closed run. A wish is not proof.
  R2  Classify each surviving claim with section 2 words only.
  R3  Moonshot stays in MOONSHOTS.oot. Never a ROADMAP 5.2 row.
  R4  Residual stays in 5.1 prose. Do not turn 5.1 into `[ ]` work.
  R5  Paste 5.3 and 5.4 from section 3 of this file. Do not add rows.
  R6  Section 6 stays a closed log. Do not reopen stale seconds,
      old module counts, import_c refuse, empty-disk registry,
      AES-exits, or “clang link is next” as this-cycle leftover.
  R7  A production `[ ]` row must pass all of these tests:
      a. It is NORTHSTAR 1.1, or user-facing fail-closed, or a pin lie.
      b. A stranger can hit it without a special env story.
      c. SHIPPED does not already mark it done with a live lock.
      d. It is not residual (R4), not moonshot (R3), not 5.3/5.4 (R5).
      e. Two reports agree, or one report plus a named on-disk proof.
  R8  Put accepted production rows under ### 5.2 only.
      Keep user-facing order. Keep oodac and library last (5.3, 5.4).
  R9  C emit + gcc remains the product path. Do not open a row that
      retires C emit or makes clang the product compiler.
  R10 Do not invent OPEN-93+ numbers in this rebuild unless the owner
      names a number. Use short `[ ]` sentences, not a new index.
  R11 Conflict rule: SHIPPED plus disk wins. NORTHSTAR later loses.
      A report must not reopen a checked OPEN-1..92 row unless its
      proof file is gone.
  R12 Do not change pin, rebuild, or live 164s / 179-module numbers
      unless INT shows a new live check on this host.
  R13 After merge, SHIPPED may name ### 5.2 as the this-cycle list.
      Keep 5.1, 5.3, 5.4, and section 6 citations intact.
  R14 Parent writes ROADMAP only. Other boards change only if a
      pointer would become a lie. Prefer DOCS for START/SHIPPED.

## 6. What This File Does Not Do

  It does not list new open rows.
  It does not rewrite ROADMAP.
  It does not certify org `std/`.
  It does not sell public install.
